# Theme elements: theme

```elixir
Mix.install([
  {:giggly, path: Path.join(__DIR__, "..")},
  {:kino, "~> 0.19"}
  ])

alias Giggly.Plot

mtcars = Giggly.Datasets.mtcars()

render = fn plot ->
  plot
  |> Giggly.to_svg()
  |> IO.iodata_to_binary()
  |> Kino.Image.new(:svg)
end
```

## About this notebook

This notebook follows the examples on ggplot2's reference page for
[`theme()`](https://ggplot2.tidyverse.org/reference/theme.html), in the order the page gives them.
Each example shows ggplot2's code first and then Giggly's. An example Giggly cannot draw yet keeps
its ggplot2 code and names what it is waiting for, so the page is also a list of what remains.

A theme draws everything in a plot that is not data: titles, labels, fonts, backgrounds, gridlines,
legends and strips. `theme()` changes parts of one plot's theme. Each part is an element, such as
`element_text()`, `element_line()`, `element_rect()`, or `element_blank()` to draw nothing, and an
element takes what it does not set from a more general one: `axis.title.x.bottom` from
`axis.title.x`, that from `axis.title`, and that from `text`. Giggly changes a plot's theme with
`Giggly.Plot.theme/2`, each element named as ggplot2 names it with underscores for its dots
(`axis_title_x_bottom`): a line, a rectangle or a text is a keyword list of its properties,
`element_blank()` is `:none`, `rel(2)` is `{:rel, 2}`, and a length such as `unit(.25, "cm")` is
`{0.25, :cm}`. It has no `element_geom()`, and no palettes in the theme
(`palette.colour.continuous`). A plot is described with `Giggly.Plot` and drawn with
`Giggly.to_svg/2`, which returns an SVG document. The setup cell aliases `Giggly.Plot`, so the cells
write `Plot.new/2`, and defines `render`, which draws a plot and shows it below the cell with
`Kino.Image`. The data in these examples is `Giggly.Datasets.mtcars/0`, R's `mtcars`: 32 cars from
the 1974 *Motor Trend* US magazine, which the setup cell binds to `mtcars`, R's name for it.

## A plot with a title

```r
p1 <- ggplot(mtcars, aes(wt, mpg)) +
  geom_point() +
  labs(title = "Fuel economy declines as weight increases")
p1
```

```elixir
p1 =
  mtcars
  |> Plot.new(%{x: :wt, y: :mpg})
  |> Plot.geom_point()
  |> Plot.labs(title: "Fuel economy declines as weight increases")

render.(p1)
```

## The plot's title and background

```r
# Plot ---------------------------------------------------------------------
p1 + theme(plot.title = element_text(size = rel(2)))
p1 + theme(plot.background = element_rect(fill = "green"))
```

`rel(2)` doubles the title's size. CSS's `"green"` is `#008000`, darker than R's, `#00FF00`, so
this background is darker than ggplot2's.

```elixir
p1
|> Plot.theme(plot_title: [size: {:rel, 2}])
|> render.()
```

```elixir
p1
|> Plot.theme(plot_background: [fill: "green"])
|> render.()
```

## The panel's background, border and gridlines

```r
# Panels --------------------------------------------------------------------

p1 + theme(panel.background = element_rect(fill = "white", colour = "grey50"))
p1 + theme(panel.border = element_rect(linetype = "dashed"))
p1 + theme(panel.grid.major = element_line(colour = "black"))
p1 + theme(
  panel.grid.major.y = element_blank(),
  panel.grid.minor.y = element_blank()
)
```

R's `"grey50"` is a name CSS lacks, so the cell writes its hex, `"#7F7F7F"`. The border is
drawn over the points and never filled.

```elixir
p1
|> Plot.theme(panel_background: [fill: "white", colour: "#7F7F7F"])
|> render.()
```

```elixir
p1
|> Plot.theme(panel_border: [linetype: :dashed])
|> render.()
```

```elixir
p1
|> Plot.theme(panel_grid_major: [colour: "black"])
|> render.()
```

```elixir
p1
|> Plot.theme(
  panel_grid_major_y: :none,
  panel_grid_minor_y: :none
)
|> render.()
```

## Gridlines over the data

```r
# Put gridlines on top of data
p1 + theme(
  panel.background = element_rect(fill = NA),
  panel.grid.major = element_line(colour = "grey50"),
  panel.ontop = TRUE
)
```

R's `NA`, no fill, is `:none`, and `"grey50"` is written as its hex, `"#7F7F7F"`.

```elixir
p1
|> Plot.theme(
  panel_background: [fill: :none],
  panel_grid_major: [colour: "#7F7F7F"],
  panel_ontop: true
)
|> render.()
```

## Axis lines, text and ticks

```r
# Axes ----------------------------------------------------------------------
# Change styles of axes texts and lines
p1 + theme(axis.line = element_line(linewidth = 3, colour = "grey80"))
p1 + theme(axis.text = element_text(colour = "blue"))
p1 + theme(axis.ticks = element_line(linewidth = 2))
```

`theme_gray()` draws no axis lines until a setting gives them. R's `"grey80"` is a name CSS
lacks, so the cell writes its hex, `"#CCCCCC"`.

```elixir
p1
|> Plot.theme(axis_line: [linewidth: 3, colour: "#CCCCCC"])
|> render.()
```

```elixir
p1
|> Plot.theme(axis_text: [colour: "blue"])
|> render.()
```

```elixir
p1
|> Plot.theme(axis_ticks: [linewidth: 2])
|> render.()
```

## The y axis's title

```r
# Change the appearance of the y-axis title
p1 + theme(axis.title.y = element_text(size = rel(1.5), angle = 90))
```

```elixir
p1
|> Plot.theme(axis_title_y: [size: {:rel, 1.5}, angle: 90])
|> render.()
```

## The length and direction of ticks

```r
# Make ticks point outwards on y-axis and inwards on x-axis
p1 + theme(
  axis.ticks.length.y = unit(.25, "cm"),
  axis.ticks.length.x = unit(-.25, "cm"),
  axis.text.x = element_text(margin = margin(t = .3, unit = "cm"))
)
```

`unit(.25, "cm")` is `{0.25, :cm}`, and a negative length points the x axis's ticks into the
panel. `margin(t = .3, unit = "cm")` is a keyword list of the sides it sets, `[top: {0.3, :cm}]`.

```elixir
p1
|> Plot.theme(
  axis_ticks_length_y: {0.25, :cm},
  axis_ticks_length_x: {-0.25, :cm},
  axis_text_x: [margin: [top: {0.3, :cm}]]
)
|> render.()
```

## A plot with legends

```r
# Legend --------------------------------------------------------------------
p2 <- ggplot(mtcars, aes(wt, mpg)) +
  geom_point(aes(colour = factor(cyl), shape = factor(vs))) +
  labs(
    x = "Weight (1000 lbs)",
    y = "Fuel economy (mpg)",
    colour = "Cylinders",
    shape = "Transmission"
   )
p2
```

Giggly writes `factor(cyl)` as `{:factor, [:cyl]}`.

```elixir
p2 =
  mtcars
  |> Plot.new(%{x: :wt, y: :mpg})
  |> Plot.geom_point(%{colour: {:factor, [:cyl]}, shape: {:factor, [:vs]}})
  |> Plot.labs(
    x: "Weight (1000 lbs)",
    y: "Fuel economy (mpg)",
    colour: "Cylinders",
    shape: "Transmission"
  )

render.(p2)
```

## Where the legends go

```r
# Position
p2 + theme(legend.position = "none")
p2 + theme(legend.justification = "top")
p2 + theme(legend.position = "bottom")
```

A position is an atom, and so is a justification given by name: `:top` hangs the legends right
of the panels from their top.

```elixir
p2
|> Plot.theme(legend_position: :none)
|> render.()
```

```elixir
p2
|> Plot.theme(legend_justification: :top)
|> render.()
```

```elixir
p2
|> Plot.theme(legend_position: :bottom)
|> render.()
```

## Legends inside the panel

```r
# Or place legends inside the plot using relative coordinates between 0 and 1
# legend.justification sets the corner that the position refers to
p2 + theme(
  legend.position = "inside",
  legend.position.inside = c(.95, .95),
  legend.justification = c("right", "top"),
  legend.box.just = "right",
  legend.margin = margin_auto(6)
)
```

`c(.95, .95)` is `{0.95, 0.95}`, shares of the panel's width and height, and `c("right", "top")`
is `{:right, :top}`. `margin_auto(6)`, 6 points on every side, is `6`.

```elixir
p2
|> Plot.theme(
  legend_position: :inside,
  legend_position_inside: {0.95, 0.95},
  legend_justification: {:right, :top},
  legend_box_just: :right,
  legend_margin: 6
)
|> render.()
```

## A box around the legends

```r
# The legend.box properties work similarly for the space around
# all the legends
p2 + theme(
  legend.box.background = element_rect(),
  legend.box.margin = margin_auto(6)
)
```

`element_rect()` with nothing set is `[]`: a white background with a black border, as ggplot2
draws it, since the box's background takes nothing from the theme's other rectangles.

```elixir
p2
|> Plot.theme(
  legend_box_background: [],
  legend_box_margin: 6
)
|> render.()
```

## Legend keys, text and titles

```r
# You can also control the display of the keys
# and the justification related to the plot area can be set
p2 + theme(legend.key = element_rect(fill = "white", colour = "black"))
p2 + theme(legend.text = element_text(size = 8, colour = "red"))
p2 + theme(legend.title = element_text(face = "bold"))
```

A text's `face` is an atom, `:bold`.

```elixir
p2
|> Plot.theme(legend_key: [fill: "white", colour: "black"])
|> render.()
```

```elixir
p2
|> Plot.theme(legend_text: [size: 8, colour: "red"])
|> render.()
```

```elixir
p2
|> Plot.theme(legend_title: [face: :bold])
|> render.()
```

## Strips

ggplot2's `~ cyl` names the column to facet by; Giggly names it as a mapping does, by an atom or
a string.

```r
# Strips --------------------------------------------------------------------

p3 <- ggplot(mtcars, aes(wt, mpg)) +
  geom_point() +
  facet_wrap(~ cyl)
p3
```

```elixir
p3 =
  mtcars
  |> Plot.new(%{x: :wt, y: :mpg})
  |> Plot.geom_point()
  |> Plot.facet_wrap(:cyl)

render.(p3)
```

```r
p3 + theme(strip.background = element_rect(colour = "black", fill = "white"))
p3 + theme(strip.text.x = element_text(colour = "white", face = "bold"))
# More direct strip.text.x here for top
# as in the facet_wrap the default strip.position is "top"
p3 + theme(strip.text.x.top = element_text(colour = "white", face = "bold"))
p3 + theme(panel.spacing = unit(1, "lines"))
```

`unit(1, "lines")` is `{1, :lines}`.

```elixir
p3
|> Plot.theme(strip_background: [colour: "black", fill: "white"])
|> render.()
```

```elixir
p3
|> Plot.theme(strip_text_x: [colour: "white", face: :bold])
|> render.()
```

```elixir
p3
|> Plot.theme(strip_text_x_top: [colour: "white", face: :bold])
|> render.()
```

```elixir
p3
|> Plot.theme(panel_spacing: {1, :lines})
|> render.()
```

## Default colours for layers

```r
# Colours -------------------------------------------------------------------

p4 <- ggplot(mtcars, aes(wt, mpg)) +
  geom_point() +
  annotate(label = "Text Annotation", x = 5, y = 30, geom = "text")

# You can use the 'ink' setting to set colour defaults for all layers
p4 + theme(geom = element_geom(ink = "dodgerblue"))
```

Not in Giggly yet: `element_geom()`, here to set the colour every layer draws in.

## Accent and paper colours

```r
# Alternate colours are derived from the 'accent' and 'paper' settings
p4 + geom_smooth(method = "lm", formula = y ~ x) +
  theme(geom = element_geom(accent = "tomato", paper = "orchid"))
```

Not in Giggly yet: `geom_smooth()` with a line fitted by `lm()`, and `element_geom()`, here to set
the accent and paper colours layers draw with.

## A default palette

```r
# You can also set default palettes in the theme
p4 + aes(colour = drat) +
  theme(palette.colour.continuous = c("white", "pink", "hotpink"))
```

Not in Giggly yet: adding a mapping with `+ aes()`, and the theme's `palette.colour.continuous`
setting, the palette a continuous `colour` scale draws from.